            <ArticleTitle>Assessing the clinical effectiveness of an algorithmic approach for mucosal lichen planus (MLP): A retrospective review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1073-1076.e2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jaad.2015.11.024</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0190-9622(15)02496-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Mucosal lichen planus (MLP) is a therapeutic challenge in need of a new treatment approach because of its debilitating effect on patient's quality of life.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We sought to evaluate a standardized treatment plan for patients with MLP. A second objective was to describe the effect of mycophenolate mofetil in this patient population.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study retrospectively analyzed 53 patients with MLP treated using a standardized algorithm. The number of MLP lesions, disease activity, and pain at the last visit were compared with baseline scores determined at the initial visit. Results were analyzed using the paired samples t test and confirmed with the Wilcoxon matched pairs signed rank test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The average number of lesions was reduced from 3.77 to 1.67 (P &lt; .001). The average disease activity was reduced from 2.73 to 0.90 (P &lt; .001). Average pain reported decreased from 2.03 to 1.03 (P &lt; .001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LIMITATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study was a retrospective analysis of a small patient population. There was no universal symptom severity scale used at the time of treatment for some patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The standardized treatment plan reduced symptoms for patients with MLP. Mycophenolate mofetil appears to be a reasonable treatment option for these patients.</AbstractText>
